Smart Table Finder (TOPA): How the System Works
Nov 21, 2026 · @Husni
Team: Martin Richiello, Guo Zhiqiang, Kaysun Molla, Husni Shahrour · Repository: github.com/C-H27/TOPA
Summary
Smart Table Finder (TOPA) shows students which tables in the Learning Center are free, semi-occupied, occupied or holding only belongings, on a live floor plan they can open on their phones. A detector watches the camera view and reports how many people and items are at each table. A FastAPI backend confirms those readings over time and turns them into a status. A browser dashboard shows the result and refreshes every two seconds.
The system has three parts that each do one job: the detector counts, the backend decides, and the dashboard displays. They only share small JSON messages, so each part can be built, tested and replaced on its own. Staff can also upload a recorded video on an admin page to see how every table changed over the course of the clip.
1. System Overview
Information flows one way on the live path: the detector pushes counts to the backend, and the dashboard pulls statuses from the backend. The admin page uses a separate path to analyse a whole video clip.
The backend sits in the middle and is the only part that decides a table's status. The detector never talks to the dashboard, and the dashboard never sees video. This keeps each part simple: if the team swaps in a different detector, such as a YOLO or Roboflow model, the backend and dashboard do not change as long as the detector sends the same messages.
2. Detection and the Handoff to the Backend
The detector's only job is to report, for each table it watches, how many people are seated there and how many belongings are on it. It does not decide whether a table is free or taken; the backend does that.
The bundled detector (studyspot-agent/main.py) works on the fixed Learning Center camera view. It estimates seated occupancy from pixel differences in each table's area of the image, which is light enough to run on an ordinary laptop. It currently watches the wall desks D1 to D6 (table IDs 16 to 21).
What the detector sends. Every 2 to 3 seconds, the detector posts one JSON object per table to POST /update:
{"table_id": 1, "seats": 4, "people": 0, "items": 1}
Two rules keep the data reliable:
• Every monitored table is reported on every cycle, including empty tables with both counts set to zero. A missing report means "no information", not "empty".
• IDs and capacities are positive integers, and counts are non-negative integers. The backend rejects anything else.
A detector can also send a whole frame's results in one request to POST /update/batch as {"tables": [...]}, with 1 to 100 distinct table IDs. If any row is invalid or an ID repeats, the whole batch is rejected, so the backend never stores half a frame.
Because the backend only receives counts, any detector that follows this format can replace the bundled one. A YOLO or Roboflow model, for example, could count people and bags inside each table area and post the same messages.
3. Backend: Turning Counts into a Status
The backend is a FastAPI application. It keeps the current observations in memory, so it starts empty after every restart, and it runs with a single worker so that all requests see the same data.
