Confirmation: why tables don't flicker
A single reading is never trusted on its own. When the counts point to a new status, it becomes a pending status, and the backend only confirms it once it has matching observations at least 2 seconds apart. Until then, the table keeps showing its previous confirmed status. The pending_status field shows what is waiting, or null if nothing is.
Three timing rules protect against bad data:
• A different candidate restarts confirmation. If the next reading points to yet another status, the clock starts again.
• A gap longer than 4 seconds restarts a pending change. This allows for the detector's normal 2 to 3 second rhythm, but stops an old reading from confirming a change after a brief disconnect.
• After 12 seconds without observations, a table becomes unknown. Its counts become null and its timer resets to zero. The next update starts a new confirmation.
If a table's seat count changes, its status returns to unknown while the backend confirms the new reading.
The belongings timer
For the items-only status, items_seconds shows how long belongings have been left without anyone at the table. The timer starts at the first observation in the confirmed interval and resets whenever a different status is confirmed. The dashboard uses it to show, for example, that a table has been "saved" with a bag for several minutes.
