8. Privacy, Limitations and Future Work
Privacy. The live API receives only counts, never video frames, and the dashboard only receives statuses. There is no face recognition; the system knows that someone is at a desk, not who. Uploaded clips are deleted as soon as the analysis finishes, and the backend keeps no history after a restart.
Limitations.
• The bundled pixel-difference detector is tuned to one fixed camera view. Moving the camera or changing the lighting can affect its readings.
• It currently watches six wall desks (D1 to D6), not the whole room, and it cannot count whole-frame people or unique visitors.
• Video uploads are sampled every 10 seconds, so short events between samples can be missed.
• State is kept in memory with a single worker. This is simple and reliable for a demo, but the data is lost on restart.
Future work.
• Replace or extend the detector with an object-detection model, such as YOLO or a Roboflow model, to count people and belongings at all 23 tables. The backend and dashboard need no changes, because the message format stays the same.
• Store history in a database to show busy-hour patterns and predictions.
• Send notifications when a table becomes free.
• Add more study areas across campus, each with its own camera and layout.
